schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Anti-Fragility (in decentralized systems)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A property beyond mere robustness — systems that don't just survive stress
    but actually strengthen under pressure, developing stronger bonds, better
    protocols, and deeper trust through each disruption.
  source_traditions:
    - Nassim Nicholas Taleb's "Antifragile" framework (concept not attributed by
      name in this article, but the definition given matches Taleb's usage)
    - decentralized-network resilience theory
    - community-organizing / mutual-aid practice under crisis
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used in this article as the fourth of four mechanisms (with redundancy,
    fault tolerance, adaptive capacity) linking decentralization to
    resilience — positioned as the strongest claim: not just "survives" but
    "strengthens."
  adjacent_meanings:
    - anti-fragility as an individual/organizational trait (broader usage)
    - anti-fragility as a systems-engineering property (this article's framing,
      applied to community coordination systems)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      resilience-vs-anti-fragility — the article treats these as a graduated
      scale: redundancy and fault tolerance produce resilience (surviving),
      while anti-fragility is a stronger claim (thriving because of stress),
      and it should not be assumed automatically from mere decentralization.
  tensions:
    - >-
      the-article-asserts-rather-than-demonstrates — "communities that navigate
      challenges together... often develop stronger bonds" is a plausible but
      unevidenced generalization within this piece.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Assuming any decentralized system is automatically anti-fragile flattens
    the distinction the article itself draws between the four mechanisms —
    redundancy and fault tolerance are the load-bearing, well-evidenced claims;
    anti-fragility is presented as an aspiration, not a guarantee.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/decentralization-resilience.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-b48196973cdf
